efficiently adding elements to both sides of the list, concatenating two lists,
indexing, and updating elements.

The concatenation operator is O(log(N)) (where N is the size of the longer
list) when it appears on the right hand side. When it appears on the left hand
side, it is O(N), where N is the number of elements matched on the front and
back of the list.

```k
  syntax List ::= List List               [left, function, total, hook(LIST.concat), symbol(_List_), smtlib(smt_seq_concat), assoc, unit(.List), element(ListItem), update(List:set), format(%1%n%2)]
```

### List unit

The list with zero elements is represented by `.List`.

```k
  syntax List ::= ".List"                 [function, total, hook(LIST.unit), symbol(.List), smtlib(smt_seq_nil)]
```

### List elements

An element of a `List` is constucted via the `ListItem` operator.

```k
  syntax List ::= ListItem(KItem)             [function, total, hook(LIST.element), symbol(ListItem), smtlib(smt_seq_elem)]
```

### List prepend

An element can be added to the front of a `List` using the `pushList` operator.

```k
  syntax List ::= pushList(KItem, List)       [function, total, hook(LIST.push), symbol(pushList)]
  rule pushList(K::KItem, L1::List) => ListItem(K) L1
```

### List indexing

You can get an element of a list by its integer offset in O(log(N)) time, or
effectively constant. Positive `Int` indices are 0-indexed from the beginning of the
list, and negative indices are -1-indexed from the end of the list. In other
words, 0 is the first element and -1 is the last element. The indice can also be
`MInt`, which is interprested as an unsigned integer, and therefore, don't support
negative indices feature. Currently, only 64-bit and 256-bit `MInt` types are supported.

```k
  syntax KItem ::= List "[" Int "]"           [function, hook(LIST.get), symbol(List:get)]
  syntax {Width} KItem ::= List "[" MInt{Width} "]" [function, hook(LIST.getMInt), symbol(List:getMInt)]
```

### List update

You can create a new `List` with a new value at a particular index in
O(log(N)) time, or effectively constant. The index can be either as an `Int` or as an `MInt`. Currently, only 64-bit `MInt` type is supported.

```k
  syntax List ::= List "[" index: Int "<-" value: KItem "]" [function, hook(LIST.update), symbol(List:set)]
  syntax {Width} List ::= List "[" index: MInt{Width} "<-" value: KItem "]" [function, hook(LIST.updateMInt), symbol(List:setMInt)]
```

### List of identical elements

You can create a list with `length` elements, each containing `value`, in O(N)
time.

```k
  syntax List ::= makeList(length: Int, value: KItem) [function, hook(LIST.make)]
```

### Multiple list update

You can create a new `List` which is equal to `dest` except the `N` elements
starting at `index` are replaced with the contents of `src` in O(N*log(K)) time
(where `K` is the size of `dest`and `N` is the size of `src`), or effectively linear. Having `index + N > K` yields an exception.

```k
  syntax List ::= updateList(dest: List, index: Int, src: List) [function, hook(LIST.updateAll)]
```

### List fill

You can create a new `List` where the `length` elements starting at `index`
are replaced with `value`, in O(length*log(N)) time, or effectively linear.

```k
  syntax List ::= fillList(List, index: Int, length: Int, value: KItem) [function, hook(LIST.fill)]
```

### List slicing

You can compute a new `List` by removing `fromFront` elements from the front
of the list and `fromBack` elements from the back of the list in
O((fromFront+fromBack)*log(N)) time, or effectively linear.

```k
  syntax List ::= range(List, fromFront: Int, fromBack: Int)   [function, hook(LIST.range), symbol(List:range)]
```

### List membership

You can compute whether an element is in a list in O(N) time. For repeated
comparisons, it is much better to first convert to a set using `List2Set`.

```k
  syntax Bool ::= KItem "in" List             [function, total, hook(LIST.in), symbol(_inList_)]
```

### List size

You can get the number of elements of a list in O(1) time. The output
size can be either as an `Int` or as an `MInt`. Currently, only 64-bit
and 256-bit `MInt` types are supported. When using `MInt`, the size is
interpreted as an unsigned integer, and the size of the list must match
the bounds of this `MInt` type, that is the size can't be larger than
`2^64 - 1` for `MInt{64}` and `2^256 - 1` for `MInt{256}`.

```k
  syntax Int ::= size(List)               [function, total, hook(LIST.size), symbol(sizeList), smtlib(smt_seq_len)]
  syntax {Width} MInt{Width} ::= size(List) [function, hook(LIST.sizeMInt), symbol(sizeMInt)]
```

```k
endmodule
```

Collection Conversions
----------------------

It is possible to convert from a `List` to a `Set` or from a `Set` to a list.
Converting from a `List` to a `Set` and back will not provide the same list;
duplicates will have been removed and the list may be reordered. Converting
from a `Set` to a `List` and back will generate the same set.

Note that because sets are unordered and lists are ordered, converting from a
Set to a List will generate some arbitrary ordering of elements, which may
be different from the natural ordering you might assume, or may not. Two
equal sets are guaranteed to generate the same ordering, but no guarantee is
otherwise provided about what the ordering will be. In particular, adding an
element to a set may completely reorder the elements already in the set, when

diff --git a/semantic.k b/semantic.k
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..226e21b7f50fc818c8fe1038d49bfa5df993d9e7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/semantic.k
@@ -0,0 +1,127 @@
+requires "domains.k"
+
+module MPY-SYNTAX
+  imports INT-SYNTAX
+  imports BOOL-SYNTAX
+  imports STRING-SYNTAX
+  imports LIST
+
+  syntax Program ::= "Module" "(" Stmts ")"
+
+  syntax Stmts ::= List{Stmt, ""}
+  syntax Stmt ::= "FuncDef" "(" String "," Params "," Stmts ")"
+                | "Return" "(" Expr ")"
+                | "If" "(" Expr "," Stmts "," Stmts ")"
+
+  syntax Params ::= "Params" "(" Strings ")"
+  syntax Strings ::= List{String, ","}
+
+  syntax Exprs ::= List{Expr, ","}
+  syntax Expr ::= "Int" "(" Int ")"
+                | "Name" "(" String ")"
+                | "ListExpr" "(" Exprs ")"
+                | "BinOp" "(" String "," Expr "," Expr ")"
+                | "Compare" "(" Expr "," CmpOps ")"
+                | "Subscript" "(" Expr "," Expr ")"
+                | "Slice" "(" Bound "," Bound "," Bound ")"
+                | "Call" "(" Expr "," Exprs ")"
+  syntax CmpOps ::= List{CmpOp, ","}
+  syntax CmpOp ::= "CmpOp" "(" String "," Expr ")"
+  syntax Bound ::= Expr | "NoBound"
+
+  syntax PyVal ::= "pyInt" "(" Int ")"
+                 | "pyBool" "(" Bool ")"
+                 | "pyList" "(" List ")"
+endmodule
+
+module MPY
+  imports MPY-SYNTAX
+  imports INT
+  imports BOOL
+  imports STRING
+  imports MAP
+  imports LIST
+
+  configuration
+    <mpy>
+      <k> run($PGM:Program, $INPUT:PyVal) </k>
+    </mpy>
+
+  syntax KItem ::= "run" "(" Program "," PyVal ")"
+  syntax PyVal ::= "applyFunction" "(" String "," List "," Program ")" [function]
+                 | "applyFound" "(" Function "," List "," Program ")" [function]
+                 | "eval" "(" Expr "," Map "," Program ")" [function]
+                 | "exec" "(" Stmts "," Map "," Program ")" [function]
+                 | "concat" "(" PyVal "," PyVal ")" [function]
+                 | "lessThanEq" "(" PyVal "," PyVal ")" [function]
+                 | "index" "(" PyVal "," Int ")" [function]
+                 | "sliceFrom" "(" PyVal "," Int ")" [function]
+                 | "choose" "(" PyVal "," Stmts "," Stmts "," Map "," Program ")" [function]
+  syntax Function ::= "findFunction" "(" String "," Stmts ")" [function]
+  syntax List ::= "evalExprs" "(" Exprs "," Map "," Program ")" [function]
+  syntax Map ::= "bind" "(" Params "," List ")" [function]
+  syntax Bool ::= "valueEquals" "(" PyVal "," PyVal ")" [function]
+  syntax Function ::= "function" "(" Params "," Stmts ")"
+
+  rule run(P, V) => applyFunction("sort_even", ListItem(V), P)
+
+  rule applyFunction(F, VS, Module(S))
+    => applyFound(findFunction(F, S), VS, Module(S))
+  rule applyFound(function(PS, BODY), VS, P) => exec(BODY, bind(PS, VS), P)
+
+  rule findFunction(F, FuncDef(F, PS, BODY) REST)
+    => function(PS, BODY)
+  rule findFunction(F, FuncDef(G, PS, BODY) REST)
+    => findFunction(F, REST)
+    requires F =/=String G
+
+  rule bind(Params(.Strings), .List) => .Map
+  rule bind(Params(X, XS), ListItem(V) VS)
+    => (X |-> V) bind(Params(XS), VS)
+  rule bind(Params(X), ListItem(V)) => X |-> V
+
+  rule exec(Return(E) REST, ENV, P) => eval(E, ENV, P)
+  rule exec(If(COND, THEN, ELSE) REST, ENV, P)
+    => choose(eval(COND, ENV, P), THEN, ELSE, ENV, P)
+  rule choose(pyBool(true), THEN, ELSE, ENV, P) => exec(THEN, ENV, P)
+  rule choose(pyBool(false), THEN, ELSE, ENV, P) => exec(ELSE, ENV, P)
+
+  rule eval(Int(I), ENV, P) => pyInt(I)
+  rule eval(Name(X), (X |-> V) REST, P) => V
+  rule eval(ListExpr(ES), ENV, P) => pyList(evalExprs(ES, ENV, P))
+  rule eval(BinOp("+", E1, E2), ENV, P)
+    => concat(eval(E1, ENV, P), eval(E2, ENV, P))
+  rule concat(pyList(L1), pyList(L2)) => pyList(L1 L2)
+
+  rule eval(Compare(E1, CmpOp("==", E2)), ENV, P)
+    => pyBool(valueEquals(eval(E1, ENV, P), eval(E2, ENV, P)))
+  rule eval(Compare(E1, CmpOp("<=", E2)), ENV, P)
+    => lessThanEq(eval(E1, ENV, P), eval(E2, ENV, P))
+  rule lessThanEq(pyInt(I), pyInt(J)) => pyBool(I <=Int J)
+
+  rule valueEquals(pyList(.List), pyList(.List)) => true
+  rule valueEquals(pyList(ListItem(V) REST), pyList(.List)) => false
+  rule valueEquals(pyList(.List), pyList(ListItem(V) REST)) => false
+  rule valueEquals(pyInt(I), pyInt(J)) => I ==Int J
+
+  rule eval(Subscript(E, Int(I)), ENV, P) => index(eval(E, ENV, P), I)
+  rule eval(Subscript(E, Slice(Int(I), NoBound, NoBound)), ENV, P)
+    => sliceFrom(eval(E, ENV, P), I)
+
+  rule index(pyList(ListItem(V) REST), 0) => V
+  rule index(pyList(ListItem(V0) ListItem(V1) REST), 1) => V1
+
+  rule sliceFrom(pyList(L), 0) => pyList(L)
+  rule sliceFrom(pyList(.List), I) => pyList(.List) requires I >Int 0
+  rule sliceFrom(pyList(ListItem(V) REST), I)
+    => sliceFrom(pyList(REST), I -Int 1)
+    requires I >Int 0
+
+  rule eval(Call(Name(F), ES), ENV, P)
+    => applyFunction(F, evalExprs(ES, ENV, P), P)
+
+  rule evalExprs(.Exprs, ENV, P) => .List
+  rule evalExprs(E, ENV, P) => ListItem(eval(E, ENV, P))
+  rule evalExprs(E, ES, ENV, P)
+    => ListItem(eval(E, ENV, P)) evalExprs(ES, ENV, P)
+endmodule
diff --git a/solution.py b/solution.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ac06f8a4305107f3bb37e8467d5a1a2e7af0af86
--- /dev/null
+++ b/solution.py
@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+def insert_sorted(x, xs):
+    if xs == []:
+        return [x]
+    else:
+        if x <= xs[0]:
+            return [x] + xs
+        else:
+            return [xs[0]] + insert_sorted(x, xs[1:])
+
+
+def sort_values(xs):
+    if xs == []:
+        return []
+    else:
+        return insert_sorted(xs[0], sort_values(xs[1:]))
+
+
+def even_values(l):
+    if l == []:
+        return []
+    else:
+        return [l[0]] + even_values(l[2:])
+
+
+def rebuild(l, evens):
+    if l == []:
+        return []
+    else:
+        if l[1:] == []:
+            return [evens[0]]
+        else:
+            return [evens[0], l[1]] + rebuild(l[2:], evens[1:])
+
+
+def sort_even(l: list):
+    return rebuild(l, sort_values(even_values(l)))
